Isn’t that a Babel kinda related question, though? AFAIK, WebPack does not transform ES6 - or does webpack2? Because if that is the case, then I need to STFU and RTFM ^^.

> Am 22.12.2015 um 23:48 schrieb Logan Smyth notifications@github.com:
> 
> Basically all from http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/6.0/#sec-resolveexport http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/6.0/#sec-resolveexport
> Star exports should not include 'default'
> 
> // entry.js
> import def from './a';
> console.log(def);
> 
> // a.js
> export \* from './b';
> 
> // b.js
> export default 'b-default';
> should log undefined because export \* from only applies to named exports, not default. This is the main one on this list that seems like a problem, because it silently does the wrong thing, and users may accidentally rely on it.
> 
> Local exports take precedence over star exports.
> 
> // entry.js
> import {foo} from './a';
> console.log(foo);
> 
> // a.js
> export var foo = 'a-foo';
> export \* from './b';
> 
> // b.js
> export var foo = 'b-foo';
> should log a-foo since named exports take precedence over star exports. Currently it throws because the export \* from tries to redefine the non-configurable property.
> 
> Local exports take precedence over indirect exports
> 
> // entry.js
> import {foo} from './a';
> console.log(foo);
> 
> // a.js
> export var foo = 'a-foo';
> export {foo} from './b';
> 
> // b.js
> export var foo = 'b-foo';
> should log a-foo since named exports take precedence over indirect exports. Currently it throws because the export \* from tries to redefine the non-configurable property.
> 
> Indirect exports take precedence over star exports.
> 
> // entry.js
> import {foo} from './a';
> console.log(foo);
> 
> // a.js
> export {foo} from './c';
> export \* from './b';
> 
> // b.js
> export var foo = 'b-foo';
> 
> // c.js
> export var foo = 'c-foo';
> should log c-foo because indirect exports take precedence.
> 
> —
> Reply to this email directly or view it on GitHub https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/1792.

